253	#### Interfaces
254	
255	Interfaces define contracts for method implementations.
256	
257	**Syntax:**
258	
259	```rust
260	interface InterfaceName {
261	    fn MethodName(&self, Parameter: Type) -> ReturnType;
262	}
263	```
264	
265	Methods use the `fn` keyword and require `&self` as the first parameter. Return type is specified with `->` (omit for void/no return).
266	
267	**Example:**
268	
269	```rust
270	interface ISprocket {
271	    fn GetStatus(&self) -> SprocketStatus;
272	    fn Spin(&self, speed: f32);
273	    fn Stop(&self);
274	}
275	```
276	
277	**Generic Interfaces (WinRT):**
278	
279	In a `#[winrt]`-annotated module, interfaces can have type parameters. This is essential for WinRT collections and event handlers.
280	
281	**Syntax:**
282	
283	```rust
284	interface InterfaceName<Type1, Type2> {
285	    fn MethodName(&self, Parameter: Type1) -> Type2;
286	}
287	```
288	
289	**Example:**
290	
291	```rust
292	#[winrt]
293	mod Contoso {
294	    mod Sprockets {
295	        interface ICatalog<K, V> {
296	            fn Get(&self, key: K) -> V;
297	            fn Insert(&self, key: K, value: V);
298	            fn Remove(&self, key: K) -> bool;
